-- CC_0351 - 2026-11-03 US Senate and US House: record the LOSERS, from AP's own ballots.
--
-- This is the pass that `CC_0339` and `CC_0350` each deferred. 599 candidate rows (76 Senate,
-- 523 House) across the 411 races AP has called now carry `result = 'lost'`,
-- `result_certainty = 'called'`.
--
-- ⚠ NOTHING HERE IS CERTIFIED and NOTHING HERE SEATS ANYBODY, exactly as in the winners passes.
--
-- ============================================================================================
-- 🔴 A LOSER IS RECORDED BECAUSE AP'S BALLOT NAMES THEM. IT IS NEVER INFERRED.
-- ============================================================================================
-- "Everyone in a called race who is not the winner lost" is the rule both winners passes
-- refused, and South Carolina is why: our `race_candidates` still carries **Lindsey Graham**,
-- who died in office on 2026-07-11 and was not on the November ballot. AP's own SC list has
-- four candidates and he is not among them.
--
-- So every row below was matched, one at a time, to a candidate on **AP's own candidate list
-- for that race**. A row of ours that AP does not list is left exactly as it was.
--
-- 🟢 SOUTH CAROLINA IS NOW THE WORKED PROOF RATHER THAN THE WARNING:
--      Darline Graham  -> recorded 'lost' (AP lists her, 46.65%)
--      Lindsey Graham  -> UNTOUCHED (AP does not list him)
--    Two candidates, one surname, one race, opposite outcomes - and the gate below re-asserts
--    that his row is still NULL after this migration runs.
--
-- 🔴 AND THE EXCLUSION WAS PROVED TO BE CAUSED BY AP'S BALLOT, NOT BY LUCK. A control added
--    Lindsey Graham to AP's SC list and re-ran the matcher: he was recorded as a loser, and the
--    count went from 599 to 600. Remove the tamper and he drops out again. A second control
--    renamed an AL-01 loser to an unmatchable string and watched the count fall to 598 with the
--    row moving into the "AP lists them, we do not hold them" bucket. Each tamper was asserted
--    present in the file before its run.
--
-- ============================================================================================
-- 🔴 8 OF OUR BALLOT ROWS SHARE A SURNAME WITH AN INCUMBENT AP'S BALLOT DOES NOT LIST
-- ============================================================================================
-- Left untouched by this migration. This is the stale-field signature, and it is a seeding
-- problem, not a results problem:
--   CT-01   AP's incumbent John Larson              our ballot row "John B. Larson" [not_nominated]
--   FL-11   AP's incumbent Daniel Webster           our ballot row "Daniel Webster" [no result]
--   FL-11   AP's incumbent Daniel Webster           our ballot row "Royal Webster" [no result]
--   FL-20   AP's incumbent Sheila Cherfilus-McCormick our ballot row "Sheila Cherfilus-McCormick" [no result]
--   MI-13   AP's incumbent Shri Thanedar            our ballot row "Shri Thanedar" [no result]
--   NC-11   AP's incumbent Chuck Edwards            our ballot row "Chuck Edwards" [no result]
--   TN-05   AP's incumbent Andy Ogles               our ballot row "Andrew Ogles" [not_nominated]
--   SC      AP's incumbent Darline Graham           our ballot row "Lindsey Graham" [no result]
--
-- ⚠ The detector matches on SURNAME, so read the rows rather than the count: FL-11's "Royal
-- Webster" is a different person who happens to share Daniel Webster's surname, and is here
-- only because of that. Two more are already `not_nominated` and are therefore correct. What
-- is left is rows saying a sitting member is on a ballot AP says they are not on.
-- ▶ Read each against the state's own candidate list before the next pass.
--
-- ============================================================================================
-- 🔴 TX-22 GETS A LOSER WHILE ITS WINNER IS STILL WITHHELD, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE
-- ============================================================================================
-- `CC_0350` withheld the TX-22 winner because AP's winner (Trever Nehls) is not a person this
-- database holds; our row is his twin brother Troy Nehls, the retiring incumbent.
--
-- **Marquette Greene-Scott is a different question.** AP's TX-22 ballot lists her, she took
-- 48.18%, and she lost. That fact does not depend on the Nehls confusion at all, so it is
-- recorded. Withholding a loss we can prove, to keep the race tidy, would be a silence rather
-- than a precaution.
--
-- ▶ TX-22 is therefore the ONLY race in the programme with a recorded loser and no recorded
--   winner. That is an anomaly worth seeing rather than hiding, so a gate below asserts it is
--   the only one - if a second ever appears, something else has gone wrong.
--   Troy Nehls' own row stays NULL, and a gate re-asserts that too.
--
-- ============================================================================================
-- MATCHING, AND THE ONE DISTINCTION THAT A REGEX GETS WRONG
-- ============================================================================================
-- Same method as `CC_0350`: key on the race (state for the Senate, `districts.geo_id` =
-- state FIPS + AP's `censusDistrict` for the House), match the surname INSIDE that race only,
-- fold accents in the generator because `unaccent` is not on production, and pin every row by
-- `race_candidates.id` so the migration re-derives nothing.
--
-- 🔴 AP'S BALLOTS CARRY TWO KINDS OF "WRITE-IN" AND ONLY ONE IS A PERSON.
--      71 rows are the AGGREGATE: `first` is null and `last` is exactly "Total Write-ins".
--         Not a person. Excluded from matching and from the seeding-gap count.
--      11 rows are NAMED WRITE-IN CANDIDATES: "Jacob Parkman (write-in)", "G. Hatch (write-in)".
--         Real people, who are matched like anyone else after the suffix is stripped.
--    A predicate on the words "write in" merges the two. The discriminator is the exact string.
--
-- ▶ ZERO AP BALLOTS CONTAIN TWO CANDIDATES WITH THE SAME SURNAME, measured across all 411
--   called races, so no AP candidate can match two of our rows. THREE OF OUR OWN RACES DO:
--   FL-11 (Daniel / Royal Webster), MI-11 (Ethan / Stu Baker) and SC (Darline / Lindsey
--   Graham). All three resolved on the first name, with no ambiguity, and in each case exactly
--   the right row was taken and the other left alone.
--
-- ▶ NO ROW BELOW IS CURRENTLY MARKED `won`, `not_nominated` OR `withdrew`. That last is worth
--   stating: not one person AP lists on a general-election ballot is recorded here as having
--   failed to be nominated, which is a real consistency check on both datasets.
--
